WEBVTT

00:00:09,490 --> 00:00:15,490
I've heard that recent studies have shown that radiation therapy for cancer could actually make things worse.

00:00:15,490 --> 00:00:17,490
Is this true?

00:00:17,490 --> 00:00:25,490
It is true. There are ambed recent studies that show that doing radiation therapy for cancer.

00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:30,490
And it's typically, I think the studies were typically unsolved tissue.

00:00:30,490 --> 00:00:37,490
It's not just recent studies. There's been studies over time that show that radiation and soft tissue

00:00:37,490 --> 00:00:40,490
could actually make things worse.

00:00:40,490 --> 00:00:49,490
So that's why I've never been a fan of radiation, unless it's a last-ditch effort that the cancer's growing too fast.

00:00:49,490 --> 00:00:53,490
Because if you... I know one of the...

00:00:53,490 --> 00:00:58,490
It never used to be this way, but about... I don't know. Maybe it was five, eight years ago.

00:00:58,490 --> 00:01:01,490
The recommendations from a college is to breast cancer patients.

00:01:01,490 --> 00:01:07,490
Now is that we have breast cancer. We need to do a mastectomy or a lumpectomy.

00:01:07,490 --> 00:01:11,490
And then we need to do radiation. And we need to do chemotherapy.

00:01:11,490 --> 00:01:14,490
Or you do chemotherapy first to shrink the lump.

00:01:14,490 --> 00:01:19,490
And then do a lumpectomy or mastectomy. And then do radiation.

00:01:19,490 --> 00:01:29,490
And then typically, if it's PR or ER positive, you'd use an astrosol or some other aroma-tasted inhibitor.

00:01:30,490 --> 00:01:37,490
To me, that's just nonsense. If you're going to... oh, radiate the area because there could be leftover cancer cells.

00:01:37,490 --> 00:01:44,490
Well, radiation in itself is a major cause of cancer.

00:01:44,490 --> 00:01:52,490
So you're not radiating a specific site. You're not meeting that site that is known to be cancerous.

00:01:52,490 --> 00:02:02,490
If you could pinpoint radiation and just hit this certain one centimeter site, that would be great, but radiation doesn't have a stop point.

00:02:02,490 --> 00:02:08,490
So it's going to go through whatever tissue it took to get to that one centimeter site.

00:02:08,490 --> 00:02:13,490
And then through the one centimeter site and then through the back of the person, it has an exit wound.

00:02:13,490 --> 00:02:20,490
So you're going to be doing just as much damage on the entrance wound and the exit wound as you did on the site itself.

00:02:20,490 --> 00:02:25,490
Well, we're going to target in different rays. So it's going to go at different angles.

00:02:25,490 --> 00:02:32,490
So that you're going to have less radiation at each individual entrance wound and each individual exit wound.

00:02:32,490 --> 00:02:37,490
So that you're not going to just destroy tissue at that site.

00:02:37,490 --> 00:02:45,490
Yeah, but you're damaging the tissue enough to cause enough oxidative stress that could cause cancer at any of those given sites.

00:02:45,490 --> 00:02:48,490
So I'm not a big fan of that.

00:02:48,490 --> 00:02:54,490
So yes, recent studies and old studies have shown that radiation therapy.

00:02:54,490 --> 00:02:59,490
It's known that cause cancer. It certainly can make things worse.

00:02:59,490 --> 00:03:03,490
So I would say yes, that is true to them.

